        "html": "<h2>AIMS OF PATROLLING</h2>\n<ul><li>To prevent any unauthorized entry onto the premises, and if it occurred, to trace it</li><li>To prevent any irregularities and if it occurred to trace it</li></ul>\n<h2>SECURITY RISKS / THREATS THAT SECURITY OFFICERS ENCOUNTER/CHECKED DURING PATROLS</h2>\n<ul><li>Trespass</li><li>Theft</li><li>Breaking and entering</li><li>Arson</li><li>Fraud</li><li>Sabotage</li><li>Terrorists attack</li><li>Armed robbery</li></ul>\n<h2>CIRCUMSTANCES UNDER WHICH THE ALARM SHOULD BE SOUNDED OR RAISED</h2>\n<ul><li>Fires</li><li>Presence of unauthorized persons or vehicles</li><li>Discovery of suspicious objects or articles</li><li>Attempted forced entry</li><li>Attempted interference with security devices</li></ul>\n<h2>PRINCIPLES OF PATROLLING/EMERGENCY PROCEDURES</h2>\n<ul><li>Be sure your radio is in good working condition</li><li>Take all your equipment along</li><li>Never use a set pattern</li><li>Walk in shadowy and dark areas</li><li>Report all irregularities</li><li>Be observant at all times</li><li>Turn back on your tracks regularly</li><li>Leave your area only to assist somebody</li><li>The principles/emergency procedures of patrolling are a security officer should always remain calm</li></ul>\n<ul><li>He needs to activate the alarm if necessary</li><li>He needs to inform the control room</li><li>He needs to alert emergency teams</li></ul>\n<h2>EQUIPMENT OF A SECURITY OFFICER</h2>\n<ul><li>Whistle</li><li>Handcuffs</li><li>Baton</li><li>Radio</li><li>Firearm (under certain circumstances)</li><li>Dog (under certain circumstances )</li><li>Pepper spray</li><li>Torch light</li></ul>\n<h2>THE BATON IS ALSO KNOWN AS</h2>\n<ul><li>The Tongfa</li><li>The Prosecutor’s stick</li><li>PR 24</li><li>The night stick</li></ul>\n<h2>CONCEALMENT SITES</h2>\n<p>Concealment sites are hiding places where a criminal or an adversary can hide and can hide stolen goods and property. The security officer can however use it to his advantage when he is aware of these sites. Examples are …</p>\n<ul><li>Manholes</li><li>Drainage pipes</li><li>Trenches</li><li>Plant foliage</li><li>Empty and unused building</li><li>Empty containers</li><li>Dark alleys Parked vehicles</li><li>Open offices</li><li>Areas under stairs</li><li>Toilets</li><li>Refuse containers</li></ul>\n<h2>PHYSICAL BARRIERS / SECURITY AIDS</h2>\n<ul><li>Fences</li><li>Windows</li><li>Gates</li><li>Booms</li><li>Burglar proofing</li><li>Doors</li><li>Counters</li><li>Turnstiles</li><li>Walls</li></ul>\n<h2>WHAT MUST BE DONE WHEN A SECURITY OFFICER REPORTS ON DUTY?</h2>\n<p>The outgoing and incoming officers will hand / take over duties by:</p>\n<ul><li>Handing over all equipment allocated to the post i.e. Radios, torches, etc.</li><li>the outgoing officer will brief the incoming officer on any ongoing problems relating to the post – if any</li><li>Officers will record hand / takeover of the post and equipment in their pocket / OB books</li></ul>"
